Compute exact eigenvalues when Lanczos terminates early

Before each step, linesearch() uses Lanczos iterations to estimate the maximum
step size. CSDP takes a minimum of 5 and a maximum of 30 Lanczos steps to
compute this estimate. The choice of 5 is arbitrary, but avoids an eigenvalue
computation that is unlikely to be useful for large problems.

However, if Lanczos becomes invariant before taking 5 steps, then CSDP forgets
to correctly set the maximum eigenvalue that linesearch() requires to be
computed. This allows CSDP to take an unnecessarily large step creating a
failure later.

This failure path will always be hit for matrices smaller than size 5 but can
also happen for large matrices with special spectra. This patch fixes this
error by computing the eigenvalue decomposition even when Lanczos exits before
taking 5 steps.

Reasoning for not upstreaming this patch: upstream is dead.

--- lib/linesearch.c	2026-09-28 11:26:02
+++ lib/linesearch.c	2026-09-28 11:26:02
@@ -256,6 +256,19 @@
 	{
 	  if (printlevel >= 3)
 	    printf("Small beta[j]\n");
+	  /*
+	   * The Krylov subspace is invariant, so the eigenvalues of the
+	   * current tridiagonal matrix are exact.  Use them, since otherwise
+	   * for small n (Lanczos never reaching j>=5) maxeig is never set.
+	   */
+	  for (i=1; i<=j-1; i++)
+	    lbeta2[i]=lbeta[i]*lbeta[i];
+	  for (i=1; i<=j; i++)
+	    evalues[i]=lalpha[i];
+	  qreig(j,evalues,lbeta2);
+	  for (i=1; i<=j; i++)
+	    if (evalues[i] > maxeig)
+	      maxeig=evalues[i];
 	  j=j-1;
 	  jj=j;
 	  goto DONEEARLY;
